// equiv_c227.cpp: the offline proof of the scene-camera selector (field reports
// R2, R3, R5, 2026-09-14). GENERATED by gen_equiv_c227.py: every block between
// the /*@@...@@*/ marks below is source/DLSS-IV.cpp's own text, lifted verbatim
// from between its [equiv_c227: ...] markers, so what runs here is the selector
// that ships. Build and run through run_proof.py (vcvars32, the mod's own x86
// code generation):
//   python gen_equiv_c227.py && python run_proof.py equiv_c227
//
// WHAT IT PROVES. Cameras are driven through the three sites the selector lives
// at: the c227 upload (Offer: NoteSceneC227's tail from gate 3 on), the draw
// gate's evidence (CountDraw), and the EndScene election (Elect), with the
// per-frame roll and the Reset done exactly as hkEndScene and hkReset do them.
//   1. With no previous camera the selector adopts what the OLD rule (smallest
//      near among survivors) adopted, over a randomised candidate order.
//   2. The phone (near 0.010, 1462 units away) never becomes the anchor while
//      the world camera was elected the frame before, in either arrival order.
//   3. A genuine cut with the phone up costs ONE frame: the phone may win the
//      live rank that frame, the election hands the frame to the world, and the
//      next frame ranks from the world again.
//   4. Night lights (small near, tens of units away) uploading BEFORE the scene,
//      with and without a cut, end every frame with the world elected.
//   5. Fourteen screen-size candidates before the scene: the eviction keeps a
//      slot for the scene, which is elected.
//   6. The water reflection at bootstrap (same near as the world, more draws by
//      the world) is elected away in one frame.
//   7. Ten uploads of the same camera are one candidate; a repeat with a
//      smaller near refreshes the snapshot.
//   8. A Reset returns the selector to startup behaviour.
//   9. A frame that counted no draws keeps the live anchor.
//  10. A fuzz of 4000 realistic frames (passes that upload then draw, the world
//      drawing the most, lights near and far, some pointing the world's way,
//      cuts and phone raises at random) never ends a frame with anything but
//      the world elected, and the election never has to correct a steady frame.
//  11. A headlight camera three metres ahead of the chase camera, same heading,
//      smaller near, uploading before the scene: the world stays live with no
//      correction (this is the case the first run of this proof found).
// It exits 0 only if every assertion held, and it prints how many times each
// rule actually decided, so a pass cannot be a run that never exercised it.
